---
title: "The handlers"
description: "grammY wiring, why the install order is load-bearing, the callback codec, and the rule that the process holds no session state."
---

Five handler files plus `index.ts`, all in `src/bot/`. They are deliberately thin:
they read the live calendar, call into `db/`, and hand values to `render.ts`.

## Install order is load-bearing

`createBot()` in `src/bot/index.ts` installs middleware in this order, and it is
not arbitrary:

```{mermaid}
%%| label: fig-order
%%| fig-cap: "grammY runs these in order. Each handler either consumes the update or calls next()."
flowchart TD
  U["update"] --> B["blocked-clearing middleware<br/><i>private chats only</i>"]
  B --> G["installGroup<br/><i>/start in groups</i>"]
  G --> R["installRegistration<br/><i>/start, guild + hour callbacks</i>"]
  R --> C["installCheckIn<br/><i>/log, tier + undo callbacks</i>"]
  C --> P["installReports<br/><i>/me, /standings</i>"]
  P --> M["installReminders<br/><i>/remind, keep callback</i>"]
  M --> X["catch-all<br/><i>answers unclaimed callbacks</i>"]
```

Two constraints hold this together.

::: {.callout-warning}
## `installGroup` must come before `installRegistration`

grammY stops the middleware chain at the first command handler that does not call
`next()`. `registration.ts`'s `/start` returns early on non-private chats
**without** calling `next()`, so reversing the order silently disables every group
`/start`, which is the primary path for binding a guild chat.

`group.ts`'s own `/start` does call `next()` for private chats, so registration
still sees every private `/start` exactly as before.
:::

The second: the handler files each register their own `callback_query:data`
listener and fall through via `next()` when the payload is not theirs. The
catch-all at the end answers anything nobody claimed, because an unanswered
callback leaves the client spinning forever.

The blocked-clearing middleware sits **first**, before every command handler,
because it must see the update whichever handler ends up consuming it. It clears
`users.blocked` on any private-chat update, since a blocked user cannot send one.
Its one exclusion is `my_chat_member`, which Telegram sends precisely when someone
blocks or unblocks the bot and therefore proves nothing about reachability.

Its write is wrapped in `try`/`catch` on purpose: this is reachability
bookkeeping, and it must never be the reason a user's `/log` fails.

## The callback codec

`src/bot/callbacks.ts` defines thirteen callback kinds, one `encode()` and one
`decode()`. Telegram caps `callback_data` at 64 bytes; the longest payload here is
an undo carrying a date and two tiers, at 29.

The interesting part is three lines long:

```ts
export function decode(data: string): Callback | null {
  const parsed = parse(data);
  return parsed && encode(parsed) === data ? parsed : null;
}
```

**`decode` re-encodes what it parsed and demands the result match the input.** The
underlying `parse()` destructures only the first few colon-separated parts, so on
its own it would accept trailing junk on seven of the kinds: `"remind:20:30"` would
parse as a valid remind for 20:00. The re-encode assertion closes every kind at
once.

This has two consequences worth stating:

- A new callback kind inherits the check automatically, instead of needing its own
  length test. The rejected alternative, `data.split(":").length` per branch, is
  the same test written nine times, and a tenth kind added later would silently
  not have it.
- **`decode` is only ever as permissive as `encode`.** A handler that builds a
  payload by hand rather than through `encode` will have it rejected. That is the
  intended direction.

## Callback payloads are untrusted and stale

Both halves of that sentence matter, and the second is the one that surprises
people.

A check-in message stays in the chat with live buttons for days. So a payload's
date can be genuinely old by the time it is tapped, and its tier can have been
superseded by a later log for the same day.

The rules the handlers follow:

- **Dates are recomputed, never trusted.** `checkin.ts` reads the live calendar
  and refuses any payload date that is neither today nor yesterday. The "Log
  yesterday instead" button ignores its payload date entirely and binds to the
  live yesterday.
- **Two refusals, two messages.** A date outside the competition gets
  `OUTSIDE_WINDOW`; a date inside it that is merely stale gets `STALE_CHECK_IN`.
  Telling someone their date is outside the competition when it is not sends them
  hunting for a problem that does not exist.
- **Future dates are refused too**, and this is not merely defensive.
  `weekMinutes` sums the full Monday-to-Sunday week while `standings()` and
  `neighbours()` sum only up to today. Those two agree **only because** no
  future-dated row can exist. Remove the guard and `/me`'s progress block would
  silently disagree with its own "Around you" row.
- **Staleness applies to the tier, not only the date.** See the guarded undo in
  [The database](database.tmd).

## No session state, anywhere

NFR-5. There is no session middleware, no in-memory map keyed by user, and no
conversation state machine. **Every button carries its full meaning in its
payload.**

The test is simple: a tap on a message sent before a restart must still work. It
does, because there is nothing for the restart to lose.

This shapes the code in ways that look odd until you know the rule. `group.ts`
re-checks whether a chat is already bound on the picker callback, **regardless of
who is tapping**, because Telegram delivers `my_chat_member` before `/start <slug>`,
so the picker is briefly live in every chat the primary path is about to bind.
With no session state to retract it, that recheck is the only thing keeping a
stale tap inert.

## Escaping, and the padding trap

Telegram HTML parse mode. `escapeHtml` in `src/html.ts` escapes `&`, `<`, `>` and
`"`.

**Escape only interpolated values, never a whole message.** The templates in
`render.ts` contain intentional `<b>` and `<pre>` tags that must survive.

Telegram first names are attacker-controlled and reach other users through `/me`'s
"Around you" block, so they are the values that matter. An unescaped crafted name
can inject markup into someone else's message, or contain a bare `<` that makes
Telegram reject the whole message with a 400, which `bot.catch` swallows.

::: {.callout-warning}
## Pad the raw string, then escape

```ts
`  ${escapeHtml(name.padEnd(10))} ${String(n.minutes).padStart(3)} min`
```

`&lt;` renders as one character but is four bytes of source. Padding **after**
escaping counts those extra bytes as column width, and the monospace table
misaligns for exactly the users whose names needed escaping. Both padded call
sites in `render.ts` do it in this order and carry a comment saying why.
:::

## Two alignment details in `render.ts`

The confirmation's progress block is labelled **"This week" or "Last week"**, not
always "This week". FR-10's backdate writes to yesterday, and on a Monday
yesterday is Sunday, which belongs to the week that just ended. Labelling that
"This week" printed a filled bar and "Target hit" for a week the user had logged
nothing in, hours after the Monday post told their guild chat everyone was back to
zero.

`checkin.ts` compares `weekStartOf(callback.date)` against `calendar().weekStart`,
**both from SQL**, and passes a `LoggedWeek` value to the renderer. The renderer
gets "which of two labels", not "which Monday", so the date comparison stays in the
caller and both week starts keep coming from Postgres.

The two labels are **the same width on purpose**. They sit in a fixed column
inside a `<pre>`, so a longer one steps the numbers off the bar underneath.

## Group binding

FR-18, in `src/bot/group.ts`. A board adds the bot with
`https://t.me/<bot>?startgroup=<slug>`, which reaches the bot as `/start <slug>` in
the group.

::: {.callout-important}
## Admin is checked on both paths

`/start@bot <slug>` is an ordinary message, so the admin check cannot live only on
the picker callback. Without the check on the message path, any member of a
several-hundred-person chat could re-point that chat at a rival guild by typing
one line.
:::

The picker binds an **unbound** chat and nothing else. Rebinding goes through the
link only, so a bare `/start` cannot be used to spam a fresh re-point control into
a chat that already has one.

One failure mode is handled explicitly: if `getChatMember` throws (suspected for
an anonymous admin posting as `GroupAnonymousBot`), the callback is still answered
before the error is rethrown. This handler has already claimed the `bind` kind, so
it will not fall through to the catch-all, and an unanswered callback spins the
client forever.
